Let \( q \) be a quadratic polynomial with real coefficients satisfying \( q(x + 1) - q(x) = 3x + 2 \) for all real \( x \). Additionally, for all \( x \neq 1 \), the value of \( \frac{q(x+1) - q(x)}{x - 1} \) equals the slope of the tangent line to the graph of \( y = q(x) \) at \( x \). Find the value of \( q(5) \).

**METHOD ST:**
Given \( q(x + 1) - q(x) = 3x + 2 \), assume \( q(x) = ax^2 + bx + c \). By the difference rule, \( q(x + 1) - q(x) = a(x^2 + 2x + 1) + b(x + 1) + c - ax^2 - bx - c = (2a + b)x + a + b + c \). With \( 2a + b = 3 \) and \( a + b + c = 2 \), and from \( q'(1) = 3(1) + 2 = 5 \), get \( 2a + b = 3 \) and \( a + b + c = 2 \). Solving \( 2a + b = 3 \) and \( b = 3 - 2a \) implies \( a + 3 - 2a + c = 2 \), or \( c = -1 \). So, \( q(x) = ax^2 + (3 - 2a)x - 1 \). With \( q'(1) = 5 \), \( 2a + 3 - 2a = 5 \), giving \( q(x) = x^2 + 3x - 1 \). Therefore, \( q(5) = 25 + 15 - 1 = 39 \).
